 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public service law, in relation to the emergency response\nplans relating to downed wires",
    "basePrintNo": "S7230",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7230-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §66, Pub Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Public Service Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7230\n\nSPONSOR: MAYER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public service law, in relation to the emergency\nresponse plans relating to downed wires\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nThis legislation would add additional individuals to existing authori-\nties who can report to electric and utility companies notice of downed\npower lines, therefore starting the 36 hour timeframe for them to be\nsecured.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends Subparagraph (xi) of paragraph (a) of subdivision 21 of\nsection 66 of the public service law to add the words \"or individual.\"\nThis section relates to the plans electric companies must have to\npromptly secure downed wires within 36 hours of notification from a\nmunicipal emergency official, this adds individual to that section.\n\nSection 2 sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nUnder current law electric companies must have a plan to promptly secure\ndowned wires within 36 hours of being notified by a municipal emergency\nofficial. This legislation greatly expands this section of law by\nincluding individuals. The danger of downed lines is real, many resi-\ndents believe when they notify their power company that the company will\ntake care of that concern expeditiously, however under current law this\nis not a requirement. Power companies are only required to secure downed\nlines within 36 hours when notified by a municipal emergency official.\n\nMunicipal emergency officials play a critical role, however they are\nhamstrung by the limited bandwidth they have in an emergency, this\nlegislation seeks to expand the critical ability to report downed power\nlines and make all individuals safer.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2021-22: S930 (Kaplan) - Referred to Energy and Telecommunications\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nEffective immediately.\n",
